<Legal
  title="Privacy · Typoless"
  description="What Typoless does with your text, which is nothing: the app corrects on your Mac, has no account and no server, and this page says exactly what reaches a network and when."
  heading="Privacy"
  updated="28 September 2026"
>
  <h2>In short</h2>
  <p>
    Typoless corrects the text you are writing on your Mac. It has no account, no server and no analytics, and your
    text never leaves the machine. Three things do reach a network: an update check, which runs about once a day and
    can be turned off, a download, and a bug report you submit yourself. Each one is described below.
  </p>

  <h2>The app</h2>

  <h3>What it reads</h3>
  <p>
    The field you are typing in, at the moment you press the trigger, through the macOS Accessibility permission.
    Nothing is read while you type, and no other field is read. Password fields are never read. Terminals and
    password managers are excluded from the start, and you can exclude any other app.
  </p>

  <h3>Where the text goes</h3>
  <p>
    Into a language model running on your Mac: Apple's on-device model, or a model you chose to download. There is no
    upload, no API call and no server to receive one. Typoless works with the network switched off.
  </p>

  <h3>What is kept</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>History.</strong> Recent corrections are kept so you can undo them. In memory only, never written to
      disk, and gone when you quit.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Settings.</strong> Your preferences are stored on your Mac in the usual place for a Mac app, and
      nowhere else.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Logs.</strong> The local log is off unless you turn it on. It stays on your Mac, in the system log, and
      is never sent anywhere.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Downloading a model</h3>
  <p>
    If you choose a downloadable model, the weights are fetched from Hugging Face. That request goes to their servers
    and carries what any download carries, including your IP address. It happens only when you pick such a model, and
    never on your text.
  </p>

  <h2>Updates</h2>
  <p>
    Typoless checks <a href="https://typoless.app">typoless.app</a> for a new version, about once a day. This is on
    from the first launch and can be turned off in Settings, where you can also check by hand instead.
  </p>
  <p>
    Finding an update installs nothing. Typoless marks its menu bar icon and waits, and the version, the notes and
    the install button are all in a window you open yourself. Installing without being asked is a separate setting,
    and it is off unless you turn it on.
  </p>
  <p>
    A check is one request for a file listing the versions. It carries the app's name and version and, like any web
    request, your IP address. Nothing about your Mac's hardware or software is collected or sent, and downloads come
    from the same site.
  </p>

  <h2>This website</h2>
  <p>
    The site is static and served by Cloudflare. It sets no cookies, runs no analytics script and loads nothing from
    a third party: even the fonts are served from this domain, because a font request would hand your address to
    someone else on every visit.
  </p>
  <p>
    Cloudflare processes the ordinary request data it needs to deliver and protect the site, including your IP
    address, the address of the page and your browser's user agent. I see aggregate traffic counts produced from
    those logs, and nothing that identifies a visitor. The legal basis is Art. 6 (1) (f) GDPR, the interest being to
    deliver a working and protected website. Cloudflare acts as a processor under Art. 28 GDPR.
  </p>

  <h3>Downloads and update checks</h3>
  <p>
    Two things on this site are counted, and only as totals: a download of the app, and an update check by a copy of
    it. Each one records what happened, which file or version it concerned, the country Cloudflare has already
    derived from the address, and the number of bytes served. No address is stored, nothing is set in your browser,
    and nothing links one request to another or to you. It exists to answer how many people took the app and which
    versions are still in use, and it cannot answer anything else.
  </p>

  <h2>Bug reports</h2>
  <p>
    From the History window, and from the menu, Typoless can open a prefilled issue on{' '}
    <a href="https://github.com/zirkelc/typoless/issues">GitHub</a>. Nothing is sent when it opens: the form appears
    in your browser, filled in, and you decide what to submit. A report about a correction includes the text before
    and after, so read it and delete anything you would rather not publish, because the tracker is public. What you
    submit is then covered by GitHub's privacy policy.
  </p>

  <h2>Your rights</h2>
  <p>
    You can ask what is stored about you, and ask for it to be corrected or erased, under Art. 15 to 21 GDPR. In
    practice the only thing that ever exists is the server log described above, and the app holds nothing. Write to
    the address in the <a href="/legal">legal notice</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    You may also complain to a supervisory authority. The one responsible here is the Landesbeauftragte für den
    Datenschutz und die Informationsfreiheit Baden-Württemberg.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes</h2>
  <p>
    This page changes when the app does. Typoless is open source, so what it does with your text can be read rather
    than taken on trust, at <a href="https://github.com/zirkelc/typoless">github.com/zirkelc/typoless</a>.
  </p>
</Legal>
